{
  "schema_version": "1.9.2",
  "id": "USN-9203-1",
  "summary": "libpcap vulnerabilities",
  "details": "Jin Wei, Kunwei Qian, and Ping Chen discovered that libpcap did not\nproperly validate the format of MAC addresses passed to the\npcap_ether_aton() function, leading to an out-of-bounds read and write. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause applications using libpcap\nto crash, resulting in a denial of service, or possibly execute arbitrary\ncode. This issue only affected Ubuntu 22.04 LTS and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS.\n(CVE-2025-11961)\n\nIt was discovered that the libpcap remote capture (rpcap) client did not\nproperly validate packet messages received from the server. A malicious\nserver could possibly use this issue to obtain sensitive information or\ncause applications using libpcap to crash, resulting in a denial of\nservice. (CVE-2026-18238)\n\nIt was discovered that the libpcap remote capture daemon (rpcapd) did not\nfree memory allocated for filter programs received from clients. A remote\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause rpcapd to consume excessive\nmemory, leading to a denial of service. (CVE-2026-18313)\n\nIt was discovered that the libpcap BPF interpreter incorrectly handled\nfilter programs that contained invalid instructions. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause applications using libpcap to crash,\nresulting in a denial of service. (CVE-2026-31911)\n\nIt was discovered that the libpcap BPF interpreter did not properly check\nthe bounds of filter programs, leading to an out-of-bounds read. An\nattacker could possibly use this issue to cause applications using libpcap\nto crash, resulting in a denial of service, or obtain sensitive\ninformation. (CVE-2026-31912)\n\nIt was discovered that the libpcap BPF interpreter did not check for\ndivision by zero in certain filter program instructions. An attacker could\npossibly use this issue to cause applications using libpcap to crash,\nresulting in a denial of service. (CVE-2026-6244)\n\nKaixuan Li discovered that the libpcap BPF interpreter did not limit the\nnumber of backward jumps in filter programs. A remote attacker could\npossibly use this issue with specially crafted network packets to cause\napplications using libpcap to stop responding, resulting in a denial of\nservice. (CVE-2026-6554)",
